  "newFindings": [
    {
      "questionId": "W01-L03-P0101-Q03",
      "questionVersion": 2,
      "findings": [
        "All three distractors are remote from a realistic singularity criterion: every robot matrix becoming symmetric, mere coordinate relabeling and arbitrary length change. Replace with plausible competing Jacobian/rank/determinant/conditioning misconceptions, while preserving the square-matrix determinant qualification."
      ]
    },
    {
      "questionId": "W01-L03-P0102-Q02",
      "questionVersion": 2,
      "findings": [
        "The zero-vector infinite-norm distractor is implausible, and undefined A0 is another elementary category error. Use parallel plausible eigenpair misconceptions (for example conflating a zero eigenvalue with a zero eigenvector or incorrectly inferring invertibility), with one unambiguous key."
      ]
    },
    {
      "questionId": "W01-L03-P0107-Q03",
      "questionVersion": 2,
      "findings": [
        "The standalone stem and formula assume that a continuous distribution has a mean. Add an explicit existence/finite-mean condition, such as integral |x| f_X(x) dx < infinity, or ask for the formula when expectation exists. A normalized continuous PDF alone is insufficient (a Cauchy density is a counterexample)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "questionId": "W01-L03-P0118-Q04",
      "questionVersion": 2,
      "findings": [
        "Random output after every observation, no intermediate conditions and universal physical safety are conspicuously false rather than parallel explanations of interpretability. Replace with plausible distinctions among traceable decision rules, predictive accuracy, information gain and safety validation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "questionId": "W01-L03-P0121-Q03",
      "questionVersion": 2,
      "findings": [
        "The source first qualifies completeness by existence of a solution. The standalone question and key omit that necessary condition, and the solution never restores it. State a feasible problem/solution-exists condition; a probabilistically complete planner cannot have success probability tending to one on an infeasible instance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "questionId": "W01-L03-P0121-Q04",
      "questionVersion": 2,
      "findings": [
        "Only the key is phrased as a guarantee although the stem asks which guarantee is denied; the others are possibilities or use of mutations. This gives a form-based answer clue. Make alternatives parallel claims/guarantees or ask directly which listed statement matches the source, without inventing a universal GA theorem."
      ]
    },
    {
      "questionId": "W01-L03-P0122-Q01",
      "questionVersion": 2,
      "findings": [
        "All three alternatives are implausible category errors (search replaces inertia tensors; Big-O computes pose; state space counts transitive verbs). Replace with near-neighbor mathematical-tool/application associations grounded in the actual summary, not unrelated concepts."
      ]
    }
  ],
